acebook', but it timed out. The timeout might be due to the page not updating as expected after pressing Enter. I should try a different approach: instead of pressing Enter, I can trigger the search by clicking a search button or waiting for auto-update, but the tree doesn't show a search button. Alternatively, I can directly navigate to a URL that filters for Facebook projects. Looking at the tree, there's a link 'All' with URL '/explore/projects', which might be the base explore projects page. I can append a query parameter to filter by name. Since I'm on '/explore' (as per the URL in the task), I can navigate to '/explore?name=facebook' to filter projects by Facebook. This is a relative navigation that should load the filtered results. After that, I'll need to identify and fork each Facebook repo, but for this act(), I'll just perform the navigation to the filtered page as the next step. I should wait for the page to load after navigation. I'll not use any knowledge base functions as they are either disabled or not applicable to this action.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Navigate to the explore projects page filtered by 'facebook' using query parameter\n    await page.goto('/explore?name=facebook')\n    # Wait for the page to load\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'TimeoutError': Timeout 16000ms exceeded.\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nI need to fork all repos from Facebook. I'm currently on the 'Projects \u00b7 Explore \u00b7 GitLab' page with URL 'http://172.26.116.102:8080/explore?name=facebook', as indicated by the task. The accessibility tree shows various elements, including a searchbox 'Filter by name...' and links like 'All' with URL '/explore/projects'. Since the URL already has '?name=facebook', the page should be filtered to show Facebook projects. I need to check if any Facebook projects are listed. Looking at the tree, I don't see any project links or articles with 'facebook' in the name, but the tree might not show all children due to truncation. I should first verify if there are any projects displayed. I can look for project containers, which in GitLab are often 'article' roles or similar. The tree doesn't show any, but I can use page.get_by_role('link') and filter by href patterns that indicate projects (e.g., containing 'facebook'). If no projects are found, I might need to search differently or conclude there are none. If projects are found, I need to fork each one. For this act(), I'll start by checking for Facebook project links on the current page. I'll use Accessibility Tree-centric selectors as required, avoiding CSS selectors. I'll use page.get_by_role('link') and filter by name containing 'facebook' (case-insensitive). If found, I'll navigate to the first project's page to fork it. Since forking might involve multiple steps, I'll handle the first project in this act. I should not use any knowledge base functions as they are either disabled (navigate_to_explore_projects) or not applicable (navigate_to_project_details requires being on the dashboard, which I'm not). I'll proceed step-by-step.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Wait for the page to load completely\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n    \n    # Try to locate project links that might be Facebook projects\n    # Use get_by_role('link') and filter by name containing 'facebook' (case-insensitive)\n    # Since the URL is filtered by 'facebook', project links should have 'facebook' in their name or text\n    facebook_links = page.get_by_role('link').filter(has_text='facebook')\n    count = await facebook_links.count()\n    \n    if count == 0:\n        # No Facebook projects found on the current filtered page\n        print(\"No Facebook projects found on the explore page filtered by 'facebook'.\")\n        return\n    \n    # Get the first Facebook project link\n    first_facebook_link = facebook_links.nth(0)\n    # Navigate to the project details page by clicking the link\n    await first_facebook_link.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n    \n    # Look for the fork button on the project page\n    # In GitLab, the fork button might be a button with name 'Fork' or similar\n    fork_button = page.get_by_role('button', name='Fork', exact=True)\n    if await fork_button.count() > 0:\n        await fork_button.click()\n        # Wait for the fork process to complete or a confirmation page\n        await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')\n        print(\"Forked the first Facebook project.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Fork button not found on the project page.\")\n</code>\n\n<stdout>\nNo Facebook projects found on the explore page filtered by 'facebook'.\n\n</stdout>\n</previous_actions>\n\nHere is the current website state:\n<webpage_accessibility_tree>\ndocument: \"Projects \u00b7 Explore \u00b7 GitLab\" {\n  link: \"Skip to content\", url: \"#content-body\";\n  heading, level: 1 {\n    link: \"Dashboard\", url: \"/\";\n  };\n\n  button: \"Projects\";\n  button: \"Groups\";\n  link: \"Activity\", url: \"/dashboard/activity\";\n  link: \"Milestones\", url: \"/dashboard/milestones\";\n  link: \"Snippets\", url: \"/dashboard/snippets\";\n  link: \"Instance Statistics\", url: \"/-/instance_statistics\";\n  link: \"New...\", url: \"/projects/new\";\n  searchbox: \"Search or jump to\u2026\";\n  link: \"Issues\", url: \"/dashboard/issues\";\n  link: \"Merge requests\", url: \"/dashboard/merge_requests\";\n  link: \"Todos\", url: \"/dashboard/todos\";\n  link, url: \"/help\";\n  link, url: \"/byteblaze\";\n  link: \"New project\", url: \"/projects/new\";\n  link: \"Your projects 14\", url: \"/dashboard/projects\";\n  link: \"Starred projects 3\", url: \"/dashboard/projects/starred\";\n  link: \"Explore projects\", url: \"/explore\";\n  searchbox: \"Filter by name...\";\n  button: \"Last updated\";\n  link: \"Trending\", url: \"/explore/projects/trending\";\n  link: \"Most star